diff --git a/docs/packages.md b/docs/packages.md
index ffe04508..30444b4e 100644
--- a/docs/packages.md
+++ b/docs/packages.md
@@ -228,6 +228,43 @@ void mylib_name(gs_rref out, int64_t id) {   /* extern fn mylib_name(out: u8[>..
 }
 ```
 
+A package may build its native library with an external provider instead:
+
+```goose
+native_build = ["my-builder", "--project", "native/project.conf"];
+```
+
+The command is an argv list, without shell expansion, run from the package
+root before its imports are parsed. Goose appends `--out-dir <package-root>`,
+`--cache-dir <build-cache>`, `--compiler <absolute-goose-path>` and
+`--include-dir <public-header-directory>`. The
+provider may generate the package's Goose module there. The language server
+reads existing generated modules and never runs providers.
+
+The provider exits successfully and prints one final report line:
+
+```
+goose-native: {"version":1,"staticlib":"/abs/libnative.a","native_libs":["-lm"],"runtime_hooks":"native_init"}
+```
+
+`staticlib` is required and must exist; a relative path is from the command's
+working directory. `native_libs` is an optional list of linker arguments.
+`runtime_hooks` optionally names a public C function taking
+`const gs_rt_hooks *`, called at program startup. The library is linked into
+`goose build`; `goose run` builds its JIT library from the same archive.
+These are build-time operations and add no per-call dispatch. On Linux,
+archive symbols stay local to the JIT library; its generated `goose_symbols`
+entry exposes the functions Goose requested.
+
+Providers which must combine several packages into one archive may also
+report `combine` (an argv list) and `combine_input` (an opaque nonempty
+string). Packages with the same combiner command are combined once, with
+`--out-dir <cache>` and one `--input <string>` per package. Its report uses
+the same version, library, linker-argument and hook fields. Goose knows
+nothing about the input's language or build system; combination belongs to
+the provider. A package uses one of `c_sources`, `cmake_target` and
+`native_build`.
+
 A package's C is self-contained: two packages that must share C share a
 repository, as `gfx`, `audio` and `ui` do. A package whose functions keep
 one state for the process, or that may only be called from the main thread,
